{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"cepivorous-jadegreen-goldfinch","creation_channel":"cli","description":"`bridge fsck` advertises four checks in its docstring. Three are permanently empty and one is\nunreachable; only `binding_drift` — added later by bug `8de5-31b4-aa13-4ed9` — does real work.\n\nRoot cause: the module arrived verbatim from the pre-rebar `digital-service-orchestra` plugin\n(extraction commit `b534f47a7f`), and **SYNC events never existed in rebar**. On `origin/tickets`\ntoday: 39,216 event files, 0 SYNC, 0 BRIDGE_ALERT. `if not sync_events: continue`\n(`bridge_fsck.py:156-158`) short-circuits every ticket dir, killing the orphaned, duplicate and\nstale arms together. The level-triggered cutover (ADRs 0026-0029, epic `3006-e198-13db-4e1f`)\nthen replaced event signalling with state entirely — those four ADRs mention SYNC and BRIDGE_ALERT\nzero times as bridge signals. The \"unresolved BRIDGE_ALERT counts\" line was never backed by code in\nany commit.\n\nWhere the capability actually lives now: orphaned -> `audit_binding_drift`'s `local_gone` cell\n(`bridge_fsck.py:338,365`); duplicates -> `detect_at_most_one_local_id`\n(`rebar_reconciler/invariants.py:121-146`, online only); stale -> the heartbeat canary, and no\noffline analog is wanted.\n\nTwo genuine gaps remain, plus one already ticketed:\n\n- **G1** `count_unresolved_alerts` (`_engine_support/bridge.py:35-74`) is implemented and correct,\n  but its sole caller is gated behind `.bridge-status.json` — a file with **no producer anywhere in\n  the tree or history** — so `rebar bridge-status` always exits 1 before reaching it. Wiring, not\n  new logic. Source events are on the `tickets` branch and durable, unlike the gitignored\n  `bridge_state/alerts/`.\n- **G2** No offline duplicate/at-most-one audit. `bindings.json` carries a `reverse` map that\n  `audit_binding_drift` already reads (`:390,406`) but never validates for forward/reverse\n  consistency. A `store_integrity` cell would be decidable **fully offline with zero Jira access**.\n- **G3** is already tracked as janitor ticket `8efe-9ceb-7d89-4a33` (`BridgeFsckOut` omits\n  `binding_drift`) — two independent investigations reached it separately.\n\nAlso: `bridge.py:6` cites \"the level-triggered reconciler cutover (epic 3a03)\" — no such rebar epic\nor ADR exists. Inherited verbatim from the DSO-era header; unrelated to epic 3006.\n\n## Decisions needed before implementation\n\n1. **Breaking vs compatible removal** — keep `orphaned`/`duplicates`/`stale` as permanently-empty\n   output keys, or remove them? The canary reads only `binding_drift`\n   (`scripts/canary_bridge.py:419`), so no in-repo consumer breaks; external consumers unknown.\n2. **Is `unknown_event_types` worth preserving?** It is the only surviving reason to walk 39,216\n   event files. If no, `audit_bridge_mappings` collapses to a thin wrapper over\n   `audit_binding_drift` and the entire per-ticket scan disappears — a far larger and cheaper win.\n3. **Should the single BRIDGE_ALERT emitter (`dispatch_one.py:353`) survive?** It has never fired\n   across the store's whole history. If identity-write rollback is handled elsewhere, G1 closes a\n   gap on a path that is itself dead — and the honest move is deleting the emitter, the reducer arm,\n   `bridge.py` and the docstring line together.\n4. **Does any operator runbook treat `.bridge-status.json` as live?** `bridge.py:5-7` calls the\n   always-exit-1 behaviour \"the contract operator runbooks rely on\"; verify before deleting.\n5. **Ownership** — epic `3006` is closed but child `8de5` is `in_progress` and its ACs are already\n   satisfied by `audit_binding_drift`. Is this the tail of 8de5 or new work?\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Decisions 1-5 are recorded on this ticket before any code lands\n- [ ] `bridge fsck` advertises only checks it can actually perform\n- [ ] The dead stale-detection machinery is gone: `_STALE_THRESHOLD_NS`, `_NS_THRESHOLD`, `_to_ns`,\n      the `--now-ts` flag, and the SYNC-gated arms of the event-scan loop\n- [ ] `unknown_event_types` detection survives the loop restructuring, or is removed by an explicit\n      decision under item 2\n- [ ] G2 `store_integrity` validates forward/reverse binding consistency offline with no Jira access\n- [ ] G1 is either wired through to a reachable surface or deleted along with its emitter — not left\n      implemented-and-unreachable\n- [ ] Any informational count does NOT gate the exit code (bug `f436`'s failure mode: one\n      never-resolved alert reds the canary forever)\n- [ ] Golden CLI help and CHANGELOG updated for any output-contract change\n- [ ] Tests that synthesize SYNC fixtures purely to exercise dead arms are removed with them\n      (`tests/scripts/bridge/test_bridge_fsck.py`, `tests/scripts/bridge_test_helpers.py:61-63`)\n- [ ] The dangling \"epic 3a03\" citation at `bridge.py:6` is corrected or removed","id":"030f-c14f-596a-4f79","parent_id":"2f79-2b32-28c5-4eb3","priority":2,"tags":["janitor"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"bridge fsck: retire the three phantom checks, close the two real gaps"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786047980725439001,"uuid":"82db15aa-d01b-467c-b03e-c2e3f2bd060c"}